// br vag3wc: `TyKind.Handle`, `TyKind.Channel`, `TyKind.Iterator` and `TyKind.Closure` are
// never constructed, so an annotation naming one of them falls through to `make_typevar(name)`
// — a BARE TYPEVAR wearing the type's name.
//
// `resolve_type_ann` (src/typecheck.bl) has arms for List / Option / Result / Map / Set /
// Tuple / Bytes, then `resolve_type_name(name)`, then:
//
//     make_typevar(name)
//
// `is_primitive_type` already answers 1 for "Iterator" / "Handle" / "Channel", and
// `new_type` is the SOLE writer of `TypePoolEntry.kind` — it is never called with those
// three kinds (nor with `TyKind.Closure`), which is what Stage 0 of the tid collapse exposed
// by making TyKind a real enum. So the kinds exist in the enum, `tk_to_ct` maps them to
// CT_ITERATOR / CT_HANDLE / CT_CHANNEL, `tc_tid_tag_at` spells them — and every one of those
// arms is unreachable. Four of 29 variants read as coverage while representing nothing.
//
// THE USER-VISIBLE CONSEQUENCE, and it is not cosmetic. `tc_check_body`'s deferred-iterable
// test asks `iter_tk == TyKind.Channel`. A bare typevar is not that kind, so ANNOTATING a
// channel breaks iterating it, while the same program without the annotation works:
//
//     let ch: Channel[Int] = Channel(4)
//     for v in ch { }
//     error[NotIterable]: cannot iterate over type 'Channel' with a `for` loop --
//       only List, Set, Map, Range, Str, Bytes, Channel, and Iterator implement iteration
//
// The diagnostic contradicts itself in one line — it names Channel as iterable while
// refusing a Channel — because `type_to_str` has no arm for the four kinds either and
// renders the typevar as its bare name.
//
// WHY THIS GATES STAGE 3 of the tid collapse (03p551 / decisions Q2). Measured at Stage 2,
// the four flat spellings are STRICTLY MORE INFORMATIVE than the tid:
//
//     var=cl tid=? flat=Fn(int64_t(*)(const blink_closure*, int64_t))
//     var=ch tid=? flat=Channel[Int]
//     var=h  tid=? flat=Handle[Int]
//     var=it tid=? flat=Void
//
// Stage 3 flips authority onto the tid and Stage 4 deletes the flat fields. For these types
// there would be nothing to read, so the divergence counter could never reach 0. Hence the
// default this ticket records: CONSTRUCT them (direction (a)), do not delete the variants and
// accept a private codegen universe (direction (b)), because (b) makes "the tid is the single
// authority" false by construction.
//
// The four variants do NOT all land the same way, and the two exceptions are the interesting
// part of the ticket:
//
//   `TyKind.Closure` is DELETED. `TyKind.Fn` is already constructed (`make_fn_type`), a
//   closure's declared type is spelled `Fn(..) -> T`, and `tk_to_ct` collapsed both to
//   CT_VOID — the variant represented nothing that Fn does not. A deletion is unobservable
//   from a test, so the evidence is the second, independent TyKind enumeration in
//   tests/test_tc_tid_structural_accessors.bl, which fails to COMPILE if a variant appears or
//   disappears without that file being updated.
//
//   `TyKind.Iterator` is DEFERRED to br qzdz2e, and stays unconstructed. `Iterator[T]` is
//   registered both as a builtin TRAIT (`tc_trait_names`, arity 1; `trait Iterator[T]` in
//   sections/03c_protocols.md, with `impl IntoIterator[T] for List[T]`) and as a concrete
//   builtin return type (`is_primitive_type`, CT_ITERATOR, and the `List[T] Methods` table
//   documenting `.take/.skip/.chain/.flat_map -> Iterator[T]`). Typecheck's adapter table
//   agrees with neither: `.take`/`.skip` infer `List[Int]`, `.chain` infers Unknown. Since
//   `types_compatible`'s container family requires `ka == kb`, constructing the kind makes an
//   `Iterator[T]` annotation reject every initializer that exists today — it would turn
//   doc-conformant code into a hard error. That is a spec decision, not a representation one.
//   The last row of this file is the regression guard for it.
//
// SCOPE. This ticket is the REPRESENTATION: the constructors, the annotation-path arms, and
// the `type_to_str` rendering. The INFERENCE path — `Channel(4)`, `async.spawn(...)`, `.await`,
// and the lazy adapter results, none of which `infer_type` has an arm for at all — is
// br e0wmt6, which depends on this ticket precisely because it had nothing to construct.

import compiler.{compile_to_program}
import typecheck.{
    check_types, tc_lookup_node_tid, tc_type_str, TyKind,
    tc_tid_kind, tc_tid_child_count, tc_tid_child, tc_tid_name,
}
import parser.{nodes, node_kind, node_name}
import ast.{NodeKind}
import diagnostics.{diag_entries, diag_entry_count, diag_truncate}
import std.io
// Bare and LOAD-BEARING despite `warning[UnusedImport]` (br xrhq1c): codegen_types resolves
// several globals it cannot import out of the MERGED program, and codegen_expr is the
// smallest root whose transitive closure closes those back-references. Removing it fails the
// C compile with `'expr_option_inner' undeclared` and friends.
import codegen_expr

fn assert_eq_str(got: Str, want: Str, label: Str) {
    if got != want { io.println("FAIL: {label} — got '{got}', want '{want}'") }
    assert(got == want)
}

fn assert_eq_int(got: Int, want: Int, label: Str) {
    if got != want { io.println("FAIL: {label} — got {got}, want {want}") }
    assert(got == want)
}

fn assert_true(got: Bool, label: Str) {
    if !got { io.println("FAIL: {label} — expected true") }
    assert(got)
}

// The tid on the LET NODE is the DECLARED type: typecheck publishes `final_tid` there and it
// takes the annotation when one is present. That makes it the cheapest window onto
// `resolve_type_ann`'s answer without exporting the resolver itself.
fn let_tid(vname: Str) -> Int {
    let mut i = 0
    while i < nodes.len() {
        if node_kind(i) == NodeKind.LetBinding && node_name(i) == vname {
            return tc_lookup_node_tid(i)
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    -1
}

fn tid_str_of(tid: Int) -> Str {
    if tid < 0 { return "NONE" }
    tc_type_str(tid)
}

// A second, local spelling of the kind — deliberately NOT `type_kind_name` from typecheck.
// A test that renders the kind through the same function it is checking would pass even if
// both sides were wrong about the same variant.
fn kind_str(k: TyKind) -> Str {
    match k {
        TyKind.Int => "Int"
        TyKind.Float => "Float"
        TyKind.Bool => "Bool"
        TyKind.Str => "Str"
        TyKind.Void => "Void"
        TyKind.List => "List"
        TyKind.Option => "Option"
        TyKind.Result => "Result"
        TyKind.Fn => "Fn"
        TyKind.Struct => "Struct"
        TyKind.Enum => "Enum"
        TyKind.Typevar => "Typevar"
        TyKind.Unknown => "Unknown"
        TyKind.StringBuilder => "StringBuilder"
        TyKind.Iterator => "Iterator"
        TyKind.Handle => "Handle"
        TyKind.Channel => "Channel"
        TyKind.Ptr => "Ptr"
        TyKind.Tuple => "Tuple"
        TyKind.Map => "Map"
        TyKind.Bytes => "Bytes"
        TyKind.Set => "Set"
        TyKind.I8 => "I8"
        TyKind.I16 => "I16"
        TyKind.I32 => "I32"
        TyKind.U8 => "U8"
        TyKind.U16 => "U16"
        TyKind.U32 => "U32"
        TyKind.U64 => "U64"
        TyKind.Char => "Char"
    }
}

fn typecheck_probe(path: Str, src: Str) ! Lex.Tokenize, Parse, Parse.Build, Diag.Report, TypeCheck, FS {
    write_file(path, src)
    let program = compile_to_program(path, 1)
    check_types(program)
    fs.remove(path)
}

// Diagnostics this probe adds with the given name. The shared global diag buffer is truncated
// back to where it was found: an unflushed entry would read as a failure of whichever test in
// this binary happens to flush next.
fn count_diag(path: Str, src: Str, want_name: Str) -> Int ! Lex.Tokenize, Parse, Parse.Build, Diag.Report, TypeCheck, FS {
    write_file(path, src)
    let program = compile_to_program(path, 1)
    let before = diag_entry_count()
    check_types(program)
    fs.remove(path)

    let mut hits = 0
    let mut i = before
    while i < diag_entries.len() {
        let d = diag_entries.get(i).unwrap()
        if d.severity == "error" && d.name == want_name { hits = hits + 1 }
        i = i + 1
    }
    diag_truncate(before)
    hits
}

// Three DIFFERENT inner types on purpose: a shared `Int` would let a constructor that ignores
// its argument and returns a cached entry pass every row below.
fn three_annotations_src() -> Str {
    #"fn probe() -> Int {
    let mut a: List[Int] = []
    a.push(1)
    let it: Iterator[Int] = a.take(1)
    let ch: Channel[Str] = Channel(2)
    let h: Handle[Bool] = async.spawn(fn() { true })
    0
}
"#
}

// ── Half A — the representation: the annotation produces the kind it names ──────

test "Channel[Str] annotation is TyKind.Channel, not a typevar wearing the name" {
    typecheck_probe("build/vag3wc_repr_ch.bl", three_annotations_src())
    let tid = let_tid("ch")
    assert_true(tid >= 0, "the annotated let carries a tid at all")
    assert_eq_str(kind_str(tc_tid_kind(tid)), "Channel", "Channel[Str] annotation kind")
    assert_eq_str(tc_tid_name(tid), "Channel", "Channel tid name")
    assert_eq_int(tc_tid_child_count(tid), 1, "Channel child_count")
    assert_eq_str(tid_str_of(tc_tid_child(tid, 0)), "Str", "Channel element")
}

test "Handle[Bool] annotation is TyKind.Handle" {
    typecheck_probe("build/vag3wc_repr_h.bl", three_annotations_src())
    let tid = let_tid("h")
    assert_true(tid >= 0, "the annotated let carries a tid at all")
    assert_eq_str(kind_str(tc_tid_kind(tid)), "Handle", "Handle[Bool] annotation kind")
    assert_eq_int(tc_tid_child_count(tid), 1, "Handle child_count")
    assert_eq_str(tid_str_of(tc_tid_child(tid, 0)), "Bool", "Handle result type")
}

// PINS THE DEFERRAL, and is expected to be REWRITTEN by br qzdz2e rather than to keep
// passing: `Iterator[Int]` still resolves to a bare typevar named "Iterator", carrying no
// element. This is a live Stage 3 divergence site — `tid=? flat=Iterator[Int]` — recorded
// here so it is a named debt with an owner instead of a silent hole. When qzdz2e decides
// whether `Iterator[T]` is a trait or a concrete carrier and aligns the adapter return
// types, these three assertions become Iterator / 1 / "Int", exactly like Channel above.
test "Iterator[Int] annotation is NOT yet TyKind.Iterator (br qzdz2e)" {
    typecheck_probe("build/vag3wc_repr_it.bl", three_annotations_src())
    let tid = let_tid("it")
    assert_true(tid >= 0, "the annotated let carries a tid at all")
    assert_eq_str(kind_str(tc_tid_kind(tid)), "Typevar", "Iterator[Int] annotation kind")
    assert_eq_int(tc_tid_child_count(tid), 0, "Iterator carries no element yet")
}

// The rendering is not decoration: the NotIterable diagnostic below contradicted itself
// because `type_to_str` fell through to the typevar's bare name, so "Channel" was what the
// user was told about a value the compiler was refusing to treat as a Channel.
test "the constructed two render with their element, not as a bare name" {
    typecheck_probe("build/vag3wc_render.bl", three_annotations_src())
    assert_eq_str(tid_str_of(let_tid("ch")), "Channel[Str]", "Channel renders its element")
    assert_eq_str(tid_str_of(let_tid("h")), "Handle[Bool]", "Handle renders its result")
    // Still the bare name, because still a typevar — see the pin above (br qzdz2e).
    assert_eq_str(tid_str_of(let_tid("it")), "Iterator", "Iterator renders its bare name")
}

// ── Half B — the consequence: an annotated channel is iterable ─────────────────

fn annotated_channel_for_src() -> Str {
    #"fn main() {
    let ch: Channel[Int] = Channel(4)
    ch.send(5)
    ch.close()
    let mut t = 0
    for v in ch { t = t + v }
}
"#
}

test "annotating a channel does not make it un-iterable" {
    let hits = count_diag("build/vag3wc_for_ann.bl", annotated_channel_for_src(), "NotIterable")
    assert_eq_int(hits, 0, "NotIterable errors for an annotated Channel[Int] for-loop")
}

fn unannotated_channel_for_src() -> Str {
    #"fn main() {
    let ch = Channel(4)
    ch.send(5)
    ch.close()
    let mut t = 0
    for v in ch { t = t + v }
}
"#
}

// The control that makes the row above a bug report rather than a preference: the SAME
// program without the annotation always typechecked, so the annotation was the difference.
test "the unannotated channel for-loop was never broken" {
    let hits = count_diag("build/vag3wc_for_unann.bl", unannotated_channel_for_src(), "NotIterable")
    assert_eq_int(hits, 0, "NotIterable errors for an unannotated channel for-loop")
}

// NotIterable must still fire where it belongs. Constructing the two kinds widens what
// `is_deferred_iterable` accepts by KIND, and a widened accept that swallowed a real error
// would be a worse outcome than the bug being fixed.
fn for_over_int_src() -> Str {
    #"fn main() {
    let mut acc = 0
    let n = 5
    for x in n { acc = acc + x }
}
"#
}

test "for-in over an Int is still NotIterable" {
    let hits = count_diag("build/vag3wc_for_int.bl", for_over_int_src(), "NotIterable")
    assert_eq_int(hits, 1, "NotIterable errors for `for x in 5`")
}

// ── Half C — runtime: the programs the representation is for actually run ───────

test "runtime: an annotated channel iterates" {
    let ch: Channel[Int] = Channel(8)
    ch.send(3)
    ch.send(4)
    ch.close()
    let mut total = 0
    for v in ch { total = total + v }
    assert_eq_int(total, 7, "sum over an annotated Channel[Int]")
}

test "runtime: an annotated handle awaits" {
    let h: Handle[Int] = async.spawn(fn() { 41 + 1 })
    let got = h.await
    assert_eq_int(got, 42, "await on an annotated Handle[Int]")
}

// THE REGRESSION GUARD for br qzdz2e. This program follows the documented `List[T] Methods`
// table (`.take(n) -> Iterator[T]`) and compiles and runs today — but only because the
// annotation degrades to a typevar that unifies with the `List[Int]` typecheck actually
// infers for `.take`. Constructing `TyKind.Iterator` breaks it. Whichever way qzdz2e lands,
// this row makes that program's status a deliberate choice: either it keeps working, or the
// decision is to reject it and this row changes with a recorded reason.
test "runtime: a documented Iterator[Int] annotation still compiles and runs" {
    let mut a: List[Int] = []
    a.push(1)
    a.push(2)
    a.push(3)
    let it: Iterator[Int] = a.take(2)
    let got = it.collect()
    assert_eq_int(got.len(), 2, "collect length from an annotated Iterator[Int]")
    assert_eq_int(got.get(0).unwrap(), 1, "collect element from an annotated Iterator[Int]")
}
